IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cda-nouns`
IMPORT `cda-seat`

-- Criminal Damage Act 1971 -- THE SEAT LAYER: worked cases.
-- One or more cases for every question in cda-seat.l4, each told as the person in the
-- seat would tell it, and cases outside what the encoding covers (an explicit "says
-- nothing" value, asserted). The expected value above each assertion was decided
-- from the text of the Act before the file was run.
-- Inputs are positional, in the order of the question's GIVEN in cda-seat.l4; the
-- comment above each assertion names every input that is not the default (no).

§ `Seat cases`

§§ `Section 1(1)`

-- I put a brick through my neighbour's window on purpose.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: an offence: another's property, damage, intention, no excuse.
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- I threw a stone at a car and did not care whether it hit; the car belongs to someone else.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I was reckless as to whether that property would be destroyed or damaged = yes.
-- Expected: an offence: recklessness is enough.
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- I smashed a neighbour's window, honestly believing the neighbour had said I could.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes, I honestly believed those entitled to consent had consented = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: section 5(2)(a) deems a lawful excuse for section 1(1), and an honest belief need not be justified (s 5(3)).
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I picked wild mushrooms from a field that belongs to someone else, and trampled them.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = a mushroom or fungus growing wild on land, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: wild mushrooms are not property (s 10(1)(b)).
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `a mushroom or fungus growing wild on land` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I broke a window of a shed that only I hold, and nobody else has any interest in it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: no offence under section 1(1): the property does not belong to another.
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` FALSE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I wiped the data on my employer's computer, without harming the machine.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I did so only by changing what a computer or computer storage medium contains = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: changing only the contents is not damage unless it impairs the physical condition (s 10(5)).
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I wiped the data on my employer's computer and the disk was left unusable.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I did so only by changing what a computer or computer storage medium contains = yes, the change impaired the physical condition of the computer or storage medium = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: an offence: the change impaired the physical condition (s 10(5)).
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- I smashed something I own jointly with my sister, who also has custody of it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: an offence: property belongs to every person with a connection, so it also belongs to another (fork F4).
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- I damaged a hired tool, over which the hire company holds a charge and an equitable interest only.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has a charge on it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = only an equitable interest arising only from an agreement to transfer or grant an interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: an offence: the charge is a connection by itself (s 10(2)(c)), whatever the equitable interest.
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` FALSE `only an equitable interest arising only from an agreement to transfer or grant an interest` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- I damaged a tool in which another has only an equitable interest from an agreement to buy.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, what interest someone else has in it = only an equitable interest arising only from an agreement to transfer or grant an interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: that interest alone does not make the property belong to another (s 10(2)(b)).
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 1(1)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` FALSE `only an equitable interest arising only from an agreement to transfer or grant an interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

§§ `Section 1(2)`

-- I set fire to my own house, meaning to kill my lodger.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage property = yes, I intended by the destruction or damage to endanger the life of another = yes.
-- Expected: an offence under section 1(2): property whether mine or another's.
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 1(2)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- I demolished my own garden wall; nobody was near it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage property = yes.
-- Expected: no offence under section 1(2): no intention or recklessness as to endangering life.
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 1(2)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I cut through a bridge support, not caring whether anyone was on the bridge.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I was reckless as to whether property would be destroyed or damaged = yes, I was reckless as to whether the life of another would be thereby endangered = yes.
-- Expected: an offence under section 1(2): recklessness satisfies both limbs.
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 1(2)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- I damaged something, reckless about lives, but I have another recognised defence.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage property = yes, I was reckless as to whether the life of another would be thereby endangered = yes, I have another lawful excuse or defence recognised by law = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: s 5(5) leaves a recognised lawful excuse or defence in place, which excuses section 1(2).
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 1(2)` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE)

-- I damaged a wild mushroom bed, intending to endanger a forager.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = a mushroom or fungus growing wild on land, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage property = yes, I intended by the destruction or damage to endanger the life of another = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: a wild mushroom is not property (s 10(1)(b)).
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 1(2)` `a mushroom or fungus growing wild on land` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)

§§ `Section 1(3): arson`

-- I set fire to my neighbour's house, meaning to damage it and to endanger the people in it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, the destruction or damage was by fire = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes, I intended to destroy or damage property = yes, I intended by the destruction or damage to endanger the life of another = yes.
-- Expected: both offences, each named arson because done by fire (s 1(3)).
#ASSERT `Which offences under section 1 have I committed` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `arson under section 1(1)`, `arson under section 1(2)`

-- I put a brick through my neighbour's window.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: one offence, not arson.
#ASSERT `Which offences under section 1 have I committed` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `an offence under section 1(1), not by fire`

-- A fire I lit spread, but I did not intend damage and was not reckless about it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, the destruction or damage was by fire = yes.
-- Expected: none: no state of mind.
#ASSERT `Which offences under section 1 have I committed` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY

-- I burnt my neighbour's fence on purpose.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, the destruction or damage was by fire = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: yes: an offence under section 1 by fire (s 1(3)).
#ASSERT `If I committed an offence under section 1, must it be charged as arson` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- I broke my neighbour's fence on purpose.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: no: not by fire.
#ASSERT NOT (`If I committed an offence under section 1, must it be charged as arson` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

§§ `Section 2`

-- I told my neighbour I would burn down their shed, meaning to frighten them.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I made a threat to another = yes, I intended that other to fear the threat would be carried out = yes, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, the threatened act would destroy or damage it = yes.
-- Expected: an offence under section 2(a).
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 2(a)` TRUE TRUE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- I joked I would burn their shed, and meant no fear by it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I made a threat to another = yes, I intended that other to fear the threat would be carried out = no, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, the threatened act would destroy or damage it = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: no intention that the other fear it.
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 2(a)` TRUE FALSE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I told my neighbour I would smash their window, believing the window's owner would not mind.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I made a threat to another = yes, I intended that other to fear the threat would be carried out = yes, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, the threatened act would destroy or damage it = yes, I honestly believed those entitled to consent would have consented had they known = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: s 5(2)(a) applies to section 2(a).
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 2(a)` TRUE TRUE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I told my neighbour I would blow up my own house next door, knowing that could kill them.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I made a threat to another = yes, I intended that other to fear the threat would be carried out = yes, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I have custody or control of it = yes, the threatened act would destroy or damage it = yes, I knew it likely that carrying out the threat would endanger the life of that other or a third person = yes.
-- Expected: an offence under section 2(b).
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 2(b)` TRUE TRUE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- I told my neighbour I would knock down my own shed, which is far from anyone.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I made a threat to another = yes, I intended that other to fear the threat would be carried out = yes, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I have custody or control of it = yes, the threatened act would destroy or damage it = yes.
-- Expected: no offence under 2(b): I did not know it likely to endanger life.
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 2(b)` TRUE TRUE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

§§ `Section 3`

-- I keep a can of petrol in my car meaning to set fire to a rival's shop.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I have something in my custody or under my control = yes, I intend to use it, or cause or permit another to use it, to destroy or damage property = yes, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I intended it to be destroyed or damaged = yes.
-- Expected: an offence under section 3(a).
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 3(a)` TRUE TRUE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- I keep a can of petrol in my car and have no plan to use it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I have something in my custody or under my control = yes, I intend to use it, or cause or permit another to use it, to destroy or damage property = no, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I intended it to be destroyed or damaged = yes.
-- Expected: no offence: no intention to use it.
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 3(a)` TRUE FALSE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I keep explosives meaning to bring down my own building, knowing it would endanger the people next door.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I have something in my custody or under my control = yes, I intend to use it, or cause or permit another to use it, to destroy or damage property = yes, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I have custody or control of it = yes, I intended it to be destroyed or damaged = yes, I knew it likely that the destruction or damage would endanger the life of some other person = yes.
-- Expected: an offence under section 3(b).
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 3(b)` TRUE TRUE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- I keep a sledgehammer meaning to knock down my own garden wall, knowing no one would be endangered.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I have something in my custody or under my control = yes, I intend to use it, or cause or permit another to use it, to destroy or damage property = yes, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I have custody or control of it = yes, I intended it to be destroyed or damaged = yes, I knew it likely that the destruction or damage would endanger the life of some other person = no.
-- Expected: no offence: I did not know it likely to endanger life.
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence under section 3(b)` TRUE TRUE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I hold a thing that the user will use on the user's own car, knowing it endangers a passer-by.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I have something in my custody or under my control = yes, I intend to use it, or cause or permit another to use it, to destroy or damage property = yes, what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I intended it to be destroyed or damaged = yes, the person who would use it has custody or control of it = yes, I knew it likely that the destruction or damage would endanger the life of some other person = yes.
-- Expected: an offence under section 3(b): the user's property counts.
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence under section 3(b)` TRUE TRUE `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` FALSE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

§§ `Section 4`

-- I am convicted on indictment of arson under section 1(1).
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = arson under section 1(1), how I am convicted = on conviction on indictment.
-- Expected: imprisonment for life (s 4(1)).
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of this offence` `arson under section 1(1)` `on conviction on indictment` EQUALS `imprisonment for life`

-- I am convicted on indictment of an offence under section 1(2), not by fire.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(2), not by fire, how I am convicted = on conviction on indictment.
-- Expected: imprisonment for life (s 4(1): 'whether arson or not').
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of this offence` `an offence under section 1(2), not by fire` `on conviction on indictment` EQUALS `imprisonment for life`

-- I am convicted on indictment of an ordinary offence under section 1(1), not by fire.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(1), not by fire, how I am convicted = on conviction on indictment.
-- Expected: a term not exceeding ten years (s 4(2)).
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of this offence` `an offence under section 1(1), not by fire` `on conviction on indictment` EQUALS `imprisonment for a term not exceeding` 10

-- I am convicted on indictment of an offence under section 2(a).
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 2(a), how I am convicted = on conviction on indictment.
-- Expected: a term not exceeding ten years (s 4(2)).
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of this offence` `an offence under section 2(a)` `on conviction on indictment` EQUALS `imprisonment for a term not exceeding` 10

-- I am convicted summarily of arson under section 1(1) -- NOT COVERED.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = arson under section 1(1), how I am convicted = on summary conviction.
-- Expected: explicitly not covered: section 4 as reproduced states penalties only on indictment (finding 3).
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of this offence` `arson under section 1(1)` `on summary conviction` EQUALS `no maximum is stated in this text for that mode of conviction`

-- I burnt my neighbour's house on purpose.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, the destruction or damage was by fire = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: imprisonment for life: arson under 1(1).
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted on indictment of what I did under section 1` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `imprisonment for life`

-- I broke my neighbour's window on purpose.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes, what interest someone else has in it = a proprietary right or interest, I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I intended to destroy or damage that property = yes.
-- Expected: ten years: 1(1), not by fire.
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted on indictment of what I did under section 1` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `a proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `imprisonment for a term not exceeding` 10

-- I did nothing under section 1 -- NOT COVERED by any penalty.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods.
-- Expected: no penalty: no offence under section 1 (nothing to punish).
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted on indictment of what I did under section 1` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` FALSE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no offence under this Act, so no penalty`

§§ `Section 5`

-- Is section 5 available to someone charged under section 1(1)?
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(1), not by fire.
-- Expected: yes (s 5(1)).
#ASSERT `Does section 5 apply to this offence` `an offence under section 1(1), not by fire`

-- Is section 5 available to someone charged under section 1(2)?
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(2), not by fire.
-- Expected: no (s 5(1) names only 1(1), 2(a) and 3(a)).
#ASSERT NOT (`Does section 5 apply to this offence` `an offence under section 1(2), not by fire`)

-- I damaged a hedge honestly believing my neighbour would have let me (section 1(1)).
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(1), not by fire, I honestly believed those entitled to consent would have consented had they known = yes.
-- Expected: yes: s 5(2)(a).
#ASSERT `Do I have a lawful excuse for this offence` `an offence under section 1(1), not by fire` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- The same belief, but the charge is under section 1(2).
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(2), not by fire, I honestly believed those entitled to consent would have consented had they known = yes.
-- Expected: no: section 5(2) does not apply to 1(2).
#ASSERT NOT (`Do I have a lawful excuse for this offence` `an offence under section 1(2), not by fire` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I damaged my neighbour's gate to stop a flood, honestly believing it needed protecting at once and my means reasonable (section 1(1)).
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(1), not by fire, I acted to protect property = yes, I honestly believed the property, right or interest was in immediate need of protection = yes, I honestly believed the means of protection were reasonable = yes.
-- Expected: yes: s 5(2)(b) with both beliefs.
#ASSERT `Do I have a lawful excuse for this offence` `an offence under section 1(1), not by fire` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE

-- The same, but I did not believe it needed protecting at once.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(1), not by fire, I acted to protect property = yes, I honestly believed the means of protection were reasonable = yes.
-- Expected: no: s 5(2)(b)(i) is missing.
#ASSERT NOT (`Do I have a lawful excuse for this offence` `an offence under section 1(1), not by fire` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE)

-- I have another recognised defence for a section 1(2) charge.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the offence = an offence under section 1(2), not by fire, I have another lawful excuse or defence recognised by law = yes.
-- Expected: yes: s 5(5) leaves it in place for every limb.
#ASSERT `Do I have a lawful excuse for this offence` `an offence under section 1(2), not by fire` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE

§§ `Section 6`

-- Police laid information on oath, and there is reasonable cause on custody and on use to damage another's property.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): information on oath has been laid before a justice of the peace = yes, there is reasonable cause to believe that I have the thing in my custody, under my control or on my premises = yes, there is reasonable cause to believe the thing has been used or is intended for use without lawful excuse to destroy or damage property belonging to another = yes.
-- Expected: yes: s 6(1) and limb (a).
#ASSERT `Can a justice of the peace grant a warrant to search for and seize something I have` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE

-- No information on oath was laid.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): there is reasonable cause to believe that I have the thing in my custody, under my control or on my premises = yes, there is reasonable cause to believe the thing has been used or is intended for use without lawful excuse to destroy or damage property belonging to another = yes.
-- Expected: no: s 6(1) needs information on oath.
#ASSERT NOT (`Can a justice of the peace grant a warrant to search for and seize something I have` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE)

-- Information on oath was laid; there is cause on custody and on use to endanger life.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): information on oath has been laid before a justice of the peace = yes, there is reasonable cause to believe that I have the thing in my custody, under my control or on my premises = yes, there is reasonable cause to believe the thing has been used or is intended for use without lawful excuse to destroy or damage property in a way likely to endanger the life of another = yes.
-- Expected: yes: s 6(1) limb (b).
#ASSERT `Can a justice of the peace grant a warrant to search for and seize something I have` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE

-- The constable has a section 6 warrant for my premises.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the constable is authorised under section 6 to search my premises for the thing = yes.
-- Expected: yes: s 6(2).
#ASSERT `Can a constable authorised by a warrant enter my premises and search them` TRUE

-- The constable has no warrant.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): none.
-- Expected: no: not authorised under section 6.
#ASSERT NOT (`Can a constable authorised by a warrant enter my premises and search them` FALSE)

-- The constable, authorised, believes my crowbar was used to damage property.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the constable is authorised under section 6 to search my premises for something = yes, the constable believes the thing has been used or is intended to be used as described in section 6(1) = yes.
-- Expected: yes: s 6(2) turns on belief.
#ASSERT `Can the constable seize this thing from me` TRUE TRUE

-- The constable is authorised but does not believe the thing was used that way.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the constable is authorised under section 6 to search my premises for something = yes.
-- Expected: no.
#ASSERT NOT (`Can the constable seize this thing from me` TRUE FALSE)

-- The police took my crowbar under a section 6 warrant.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the property has come into the possession of the police under section 6 = yes.
-- Expected: the 1897 Act applies (s 6(3)).
#ASSERT `What law decides what happens to what the police have taken from me` TRUE EQUALS `the Police (Property) Act 1897 applies, as it applies to property that has come into the possession of the police in the circumstances mentioned in that Act`

-- The police took my crowbar some other way -- NOT COVERED.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): none.
-- Expected: explicitly not covered: section 6(3) is silent about property taken other than under section 6.
#ASSERT `What law decides what happens to what the police have taken from me` FALSE EQUALS `section 6(3) says nothing about this`

§§ `Section 7`

-- The owner and I dispute who owns the damaged wall; I am charged with criminal damage.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): a rule of law says magistrates' courts cannot try offences where a dispute of title to property is involved = yes, I am charged with an offence under this Act or another offence of destroying or damaging property = yes.
-- Expected: the magistrates' court can try it (s 7(2)).
#ASSERT `Can a magistrates' court try me despite a dispute over who owns the property` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `that rule of law does not preclude a magistrates' court from trying the offence`

-- The same rule, but I am charged with an unrelated offence -- NOT COVERED.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): a rule of law says magistrates' courts cannot try offences where a dispute of title to property is involved = yes.
-- Expected: explicitly not covered: s 7(2) reaches only offences under the Act or of destroying or damaging property.
#ASSERT `Can a magistrates' court try me despite a dispute over who owns the property` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `section 7(2) says nothing about this`

§§ `Section 9`

-- In civil proceedings to recover property I am asked a question whose answer may incriminate me.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the proceedings are for the recovery or administration of property, for the execution of a trust or for an account of property or dealings with property = yes, a question is put to me in those proceedings = yes, answering or complying may incriminate me or my spouse or civil partner of an offence under this Act = yes.
-- Expected: not excused (s 9).
#ASSERT `Must I answer a question or obey an order even though it may incriminate me or my spouse` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not excused by reason that to answer or comply may incriminate`

-- The same question in a criminal trial -- NOT COVERED.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): a question is put to me in those proceedings = yes, answering or complying may incriminate me or my spouse or civil partner of an offence under this Act = yes.
-- Expected: explicitly not covered: s 9 reaches only the civil proceedings it names.
#ASSERT `Must I answer a question or obey an order even though it may incriminate me or my spouse` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `section 9 says nothing about this`

-- I answered in the civil case; the prosecution offers my answer against me.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): my statement or admission was made in answering a question put, or complying with an order made, in such proceedings = yes, it is offered in proceedings for an offence under this Act = yes, it is offered against = the person who made it.
-- Expected: not admissible (s 9).
#ASSERT `Can what I said in answering or complying be used in evidence against me or my spouse` TRUE TRUE `the person who made it` FALSE EQUALS `not admissible in evidence`

-- The prosecution offers it against my spouse, whom I married before I made it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): my statement or admission was made in answering a question put, or complying with an order made, in such proceedings = yes, it is offered in proceedings for an offence under this Act = yes, it is offered against = the spouse or civil partner of the person who made it.
-- Expected: not admissible (s 9).
#ASSERT `Can what I said in answering or complying be used in evidence against me or my spouse` TRUE TRUE `the spouse or civil partner of the person who made it` FALSE EQUALS `not admissible in evidence`

-- The prosecution offers it against my spouse, whom I married after I made it -- the proviso.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): my statement or admission was made in answering a question put, or complying with an order made, in such proceedings = yes, it is offered in proceedings for an offence under this Act = yes, it is offered against = the spouse or civil partner of the person who made it, my spouse or civil partner married me or became my civil partner after I made it = yes.
-- Expected: explicitly not covered: the proviso lifts the bar for a spouse who married afterwards.
#ASSERT `Can what I said in answering or complying be used in evidence against me or my spouse` TRUE TRUE `the spouse or civil partner of the person who made it` TRUE EQUALS `section 9 says nothing about this`

§§ `Section 10`

-- A tamed fox I kept as a pet.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = a wild creature that has been tamed or is ordinarily kept in captivity.
-- Expected: property (s 10(1)(a)).
#ASSERT `Is the thing property within the Act` `a wild creature that has been tamed or is ordinarily kept in captivity`

-- A wild fox that no one has caught.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = another wild creature or a carcass, not taken into possession.
-- Expected: not property (s 10(1)(a): 'if, but only if').
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the thing property within the Act` `another wild creature or a carcass, not taken into possession`)

-- Money.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = money.
-- Expected: property.
#ASSERT `Is the thing property within the Act` `money`

-- An idea.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = something that is not of a tangible nature.
-- Expected: not property: not tangible.
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the thing property within the Act` `something that is not of a tangible nature`)

-- A van that my partner has custody of.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, someone else has custody or control of it = yes.
-- Expected: yes (s 10(2)(a)).
#ASSERT `Is the property treated as belonging to someone else` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- A van that nobody else is connected with.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods.
-- Expected: no.
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the property treated as belonging to someone else` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` FALSE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- A van I have custody of.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, I have custody or control of it = yes.
-- Expected: yes (s 10(2)(a)).
#ASSERT `Is the property treated as belonging to me` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` TRUE `no proprietary right or interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- A van in which I have only an equitable interest from an agreement to buy it.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): what the thing is = an ordinary physical thing, land or goods, what interest I have in it = only an equitable interest arising only from an agreement to transfer or grant an interest.
-- Expected: no (s 10(2)(b) carve-out).
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the property treated as belonging to me` `an ordinary physical thing, land or goods` FALSE `only an equitable interest arising only from an agreement to transfer or grant an interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE)

-- I corrupted files only.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I did so only by changing what a computer or computer storage medium contains = yes.
-- Expected: not damage (s 10(5)).
#ASSERT NOT (`Is what I did to the computer or storage medium damage within the Act` TRUE TRUE FALSE)

-- I corrupted files and the drive was left unusable.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): I destroyed or damaged it = yes, I did so only by changing what a computer or computer storage medium contains = yes, the change impaired the physical condition of the computer or storage medium = yes.
-- Expected: damage (s 10(5)).
#ASSERT `Is what I did to the computer or storage medium damage within the Act` TRUE TRUE TRUE

§§ `Section 11`

-- I set a fire in 2020, long after the Act came into force.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the day I did it = Date 5 3 2020.
-- Expected: abolished (s 11(1)).
#ASSERT `Can I be charged with the common law offence of arson` (Date 5 3 2020) EQUALS `abolished by section 11(1)`

-- I set a fire on 13 October 1971, the last day before the Act came into force -- NOT COVERED.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the day I did it = Date 13 10 1971.
-- Expected: explicitly not covered: the abolition takes effect with the Act.
#ASSERT `Can I be charged with the common law offence of arson` (Date 13 10 1971) EQUALS `section 11(1) says nothing about an act done before this Act came into force`

-- An old procedural enactment repealed by the Act still governs how I am tried, as my offence was committed before the repeal.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the enactment relates to procedure or to the jurisdictional powers of a court = yes, the enactment is repealed by section 11 or by an order made under it = yes, the offence was committed before the repeal takes effect = yes.
-- Expected: it continues to operate (s 11(11)).
#ASSERT `Do the old rules of procedure or jurisdiction still apply to what I did before the repeal` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `that enactment continues to operate in relation to the offence and to proceedings for it`

-- The same enactment, but I committed my offence after the repeal took effect -- NOT COVERED.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the enactment relates to procedure or to the jurisdictional powers of a court = yes, the enactment is repealed by section 11 or by an order made under it = yes.
-- Expected: explicitly not covered: s 11(11) saves the enactment only for earlier offences.
#ASSERT `Do the old rules of procedure or jurisdiction still apply to what I did before the repeal` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `section 11(11) says nothing about this`

§§ `Section 12`

-- I did it on 13 October 1971.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the day I did it = Date 13 10 1971.
-- Expected: no: the Act comes into force at the expiration of three months beginning with the day it was passed, 14 July 1971 (s 12(1); fork F7).
#ASSERT NOT (`Was the Act in force on the day I did it` (Date 13 10 1971))

-- I did it on 14 October 1971.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the day I did it = Date 14 10 1971.
-- Expected: yes: first day in force.
#ASSERT `Was the Act in force on the day I did it` (Date 14 10 1971)

-- It happened in Cardiff.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the place = Wales.
-- Expected: extends (long title).
#ASSERT `Does the Act's text reach the place where I did it` `Wales` EQUALS `they extend there`

-- It happened in Glasgow.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the place = Scotland.
-- Expected: does not extend (s 12(3)).
#ASSERT `Does the Act's text reach the place where I did it` `Scotland` EQUALS `they do not extend there`

-- It happened somewhere else -- NOT COVERED.
-- Facts stated (all others default to no): the place = somewhere else.
-- Expected: explicitly not covered.
#ASSERT `Does the Act's text reach the place where I did it` `somewhere else` EQUALS `this text says nothing about that place`
